---
import Layout from '../layouts/Layout.astro';
import Header from '../components/Header.astro';
import Footer from '../components/Footer.astro';
---

<Layout
  title="Bitify Is Gone: What to Use Instead in 2026 - Mobazha"
  description="Bitify shut down in 2025 after more than a decade. What happened, what replaced it, and where you can still buy and sell with crypto today."
>
  <Header />
  <main class="pt-32 pb-20">
    <article class="mx-auto max-w-3xl px-6 lg:px-8">
      <h1 class="text-4xl font-extrabold tracking-tight text-[var(--color-text-primary)] sm:text-5xl">
        Bitify is gone. Here is what actually works instead.
      </h1>
      <p class="mt-6 text-lg text-[var(--color-text-body)]">
        Bitify closed in 2025. If you are reading this, you probably had an account, or you were about to open one, and now you need somewhere else to buy and sell with crypto.
      </p>
      <p class="mt-4 text-[var(--color-text-body)]">
        This page is not a list of ten random apps. It explains what happened to Bitify, what the real options are now, and what to check before you trust a replacement with your money.
      </p>

      <h2 class="mt-12 text-2xl font-bold text-[var(--color-text-primary)]">What Bitify was</h2>
      <p class="mt-4 text-[var(--color-text-body)]">
        Bitify started in September 2013 as CryptoThrift, a side project by a single founder in Australia. It grew into a Bitcoin and Litecoin marketplace with auctions, a built-in escrow service, and a 2.5% fee on sold items.
      </p>
      <p class="mt-4 text-[var(--color-text-body)]">
        It became known for something narrower too: discounted gift cards. That category brought volume, and it brought a different kind of customer.
      </p>
      <p class="mt-4 text-[var(--color-text-body)]">
        The site was put up for sale in 2024. Users reported being unable to withdraw funds. By early 2025 it was offline. The shutdown page is still up, and it still says the domain and the code are for sale.
      </p>

      <h2 class="mt-12 text-2xl font-bold text-[var(--color-text-primary)]">Why the replacement is harder than it looks</h2>
      <p class="mt-4 text-[var(--color-text-body)]">
        If you only bought gift cards, this is easy. Bitrefill, Coinsbee and TheBitcoinCompany still sell gift cards for crypto, and they have been running for years.
      </p>
      <p class="mt-4 text-[var(--color-text-body)]">
        Everything else is the problem. There is no marketplace left where a normal person can list a used camera, sell something they made, or run a small shop and get paid in bitcoin.
      </p>
      <p class="mt-4 text-[var(--color-text-body)]">
        The platforms that could have replaced it are gone too. Paxful shut down in 2025 after a US enforcement action. NoOnes, which took in Paxful's users, wound down in August 2026.
      </p>

      <h2 class="mt-12 text-2xl font-bold text-[var(--color-text-primary)]">What is actually left</h2>
      <ul class="mt-4 space-y-3 text-[var(--color-text-body)]">
        <li><strong>Gift card shops.</strong> Bitrefill, Coinsbee, TheBitcoinCompany. Fast, reliable, and limited to buying new cards.</li>
        <li><strong>Escrow services.</strong> Useful if you already found a counterparty somewhere else. They do not help you find anyone.</li>
        <li><strong>Marketplaces.</strong> Particl Marketplace, Zano BaZar, and Mobazha. This is the smallest group, and the only one that solves the actual problem.</li>
      </ul>

      <h2 class="mt-12 text-2xl font-bold text-[var(--color-text-primary)]">What Mobazha is</h2>
      <p class="mt-4 text-[var(--color-text-body)]">
        Mobazha is a marketplace built on the OpenBazaar codebase by members of that original community. When OB1 walked away from the project in 2021, they kept the code and kept building.
      </p>
      <p class="mt-4 text-[var(--color-text-body)]">
        You can buy and sell physical goods, second-hand items, digital goods, and gift cards. Sellers open their own store and keep their customer list. There is no KYC. Payments use 2-of-3 multisig, so no single party can move the money alone. Disputes go to independent arbitrators.
      </p>
      <p class="mt-4 text-[var(--color-text-body)]">
        If you do not have stock, you can list products from suppliers who hold inventory in several countries and handle storage and shipping. You sell, they ship.
      </p>

      <h2 class="mt-12 text-2xl font-bold text-[var(--color-text-primary)]">What to check before trusting anyone, including us</h2>
      <p class="mt-4 text-[var(--color-text-body)]">
        Who holds the money. If it sits in a platform account, you are trusting a company to stay honest.
      </p>
      <p class="mt-4 text-[var(--color-text-body)]">
        What happens when the buyer says the box was empty and the seller has a tracking number. A refund policy is not a dispute process.
      </p>
      <p class="mt-4 text-[var(--color-text-body)]">
        Whether you can export your listings and customers. If you cannot, you are renting.
      </p>

      <h2 class="mt-12 text-2xl font-bold text-[var(--color-text-primary)]">How to start</h2>
      <p class="mt-4 text-[var(--color-text-body)]">
        Create a store, list one item, and share the link. That is the whole onboarding. You do not need an ID document, and you do not need to fund an account.
      </p>
      <p class="mt-4 text-[var(--color-text-body)]">
        If you were on Bitify for gift cards, start there. If you were there for something else, list that instead. The marketplace does not care what the item is, as long as selling it is legal where you are.
      </p>
      <p class="mt-8">
        <a class="font-semibold text-[var(--color-brand-light)] underline" href="https://app.mobazha.org/">Open a store on Mobazha</a>
      </p>
    </article>
  </main>
  <Footer />
</Layout>
